      "message": "\u003e This pattern looks unnatural: typically to have a body in the test that expresses the expectations.\n\u003e \n\u003e In this case, the body is empty and you just give success if there are no exceptions. All the other tests do the same.\n\u003e \n\u003e By reading the implementation of PluginInjectsInjectorModule at L50 you can then see the expectations.\n\u003e \n\u003e It would be more readable if the module just exposes the values and you put the expectations in the body here.\n\nI completely agree on the problems. I didn\u0027t love how all this test code looked, but I wasn\u0027t sure how to improve it.\n\nHowever, your approach doesn\u0027t actually do the same thing that mine does. You\u0027re checking injection after the plugin has finished installing all modules vs I\u0027m checking within each module.\n\nWe know from our implementation that if I specify a plugin HttpModule, we will try to get an instance of it from the plugin\u0027s sysModule. So from that, your suggested `assertThatInjectedPluginName()` would be doing an equivalent thing. But... now we\u0027re not actually testing what happens in ServerPlugin, we\u0027re just trying to duplicate it. Instead, I can do some Guice magic to pass test a consumer (or several chained together) into the Module\u0027s constructor and then run that `Consumer.accept()` in the test plugin Module\u0027s `configure()`.\n\nHopefully you find the new version more readable and maybe you have more ideas for how to improve it further.",
      "parentUuid": "51d2ac61_96afb3a0",
      "range": {
